/-!
# The natural alternating-six distance-four argument

This subtree owns the source-(5.12) exclusion on the literal characteristic
replacement, independently of the distance-greater-than-four proof.
`SmallSlice` proves the critical-image exclusion and selected derived bound
for nonabelian small-slice paths, retaining every radical image and both
central covers, and applies these to the original terminal argument.
`Kernel` identifies the common midpoint kernel's derived line in that branch,
puts the full moving cover in the whole midpoint core's derived subgroup,
and removes the endpoint-commutator premise from the terminal application.
`ResidualProduct` strengthens the whole-core obstruction to
|[C,C]| |[Y,C]| > 4, for the full midpoint residual core C and selected
kernel Y. Its terminal consumer only needs the opposite product bound;
no containment of [Y,C] in [C,C] is assumed.
`Selection` constructs a nonabelian large-slice critical path from any
nonabelian selected path, using reciprocal endpoint control without a
point-core-image or universal-escape assumption.
`LargeImage` proves whole-radius escape for large critical images, then
constructs forward eligibility and selected-span nonabelianness at distance
four. Its small-slice application forces both critical images to have order
two without assuming nonabelianness.
`RestrictedSeed` constructs a full endpoint restriction with its central
attachment and exact critical commutator. It normalizes each backward moving
kernel joined with the starting seed, and supplies a conjugate backward
critical path with these estimates in the large-image branch.
`InitialSelection` supplies a nonabelian large-slice path for the full
point-core image on either cover. Otherwise absence of initial selection
forces every critical image to have order two and every nearby whole-radius
image into a conjugate of the three-disjoint-transposition group. This bounds
the radius image, not the radical image.
`SmallRadius` uses that image bound to show that every whole midpoint radius
fixes a subgroup of index at most four in the full starting seed and of
index at most two in the full critical kernel. Both bounds transport along
the literal critical path and apply at every component vertex. They retain
the entire central attachment and do not assert a commutator order bound.
`SmallRadiusAction` proves the corresponding full-kernel commutator has order
at most four. When its order exceeds two, it contains the whole starting-seed
commutator modulo the critical line. The literal replacement application
retains both critical indices and the kernel fixed-index bound.
`PredecessorResidual` bounds the full critical kernel's commutator with the
whole midpoint two-core by eight on every critical path, without a slice or
radical-image assumption. A small predecessor slice then gives residual
fixedness and exact conjugation transport for every subgroup of that core.
Its original-replacement application constructs the same residual element
moving the last edge to the first and retains the full-seed transport.
`ResidualRotation` makes the full critical kernel centralize the entire
midpoint radius under a small predecessor slice. It identifies that radius's
endpoint image, full-seed displacement and fixed kernel exactly. Nonzero
kernel movement constructs the literal rotated critical path; in the uniformly
small-image alternative, alignment with the old critical line and endpoint
center is impossible. Its original initial-selection consumer retains the
small-slice and aligned-moving-seed premises for source construction.
`NonnormalSelection` constructs the nonzero aligned kernel line from source
normalization failure and supplies it from an eligible generating edge.
Its normal-core argument applies to any eligible neighbor with a trapped
orbit span. A small predecessor slice alone now supplies initial selection:
a trapped whole radius supplies such a neighbor, and an escaping radius
constructs a reciprocal path that makes the forward seed eligible. This
holds for every radical image and both covers. Transport and reversal leave
only the alternative in which both endpoint seeds have midpoint-slice index
greater than two on every critical path.
`CubeSelection` closes initial selection for the three-pair radical image.
Its paired-kernel construction either gives a nonabelian forward span or a
backward path with a small predecessor slice. The maximal-image theorem
then supplies initial selection for every nonsylow original radical, on both
covers.
`ElementarySelection` proves nonabelianness of a strict forward span in the
mixed-plane image by the actual relative-normalizer trapped-orbit estimate.
Mixed-plane radius escape constructs a reciprocal path that supplies that
strict slice when initial selection is absent. Thus all order-four images
and all elementary radical images now admit initial selection. The literal
original-replacement consumer retains only non-elementary images of orders
sixteen and eight, with Sylow restriction indices one and two. Initial
selection for these last two images is supplied by the modules below. The
independent selected terminal family and final assembly remain obligations.
`NonelementaryMovers` identifies the radical normalizer with the literal
restricted original Sylow and constructs its unique maximum small mover
already above distance two. It transports its fixed indices, small-actor
control and quadratic action through each actual edge embedding.
`SixteenUnequal` excludes a trapped strict seed with unequal neighboring
movers in image sixteen by full-omega comparison and residual generation.
Its backward critical path supplies initial selection for a strict unequal
forward slice, or for a small forward slice with a strict unequal neighbor.
`EqualKernel` derives the elementary action image of three generating
seeds from their derived-subgroup containment in the critical transposition
seed. This bounds the full critical-kernel commutator without assuming the
whole seed join is abelian. `SixteenEqual` applies that bound to the paired
equal-mover configuration, constructs the backward path and completes
initial selection for every order-sixteen radical image. Its original
consumer leaves only non-elementary image eight with original Sylow and
restriction index two.
`EightMovers` constructs the unique actual image-eight mover and its edge
transport above distance two, retaining the larger relative normalizer.
`EightGenerating` supplies one generating edge for two endpoint seeds and
one residual transporter belonging to the paired movers.
`EightEqual` uses the full-kernel estimate on the three-seed join to exclude
trapping with equal movers. `EightUnequal` uses the neighboring mover's
fixed index eight and whole-seed fixer to exclude trapping with unequal
movers. Both construct backward critical paths with every overlap and the
same generating edge. `EightBaumann` excludes the two-transposition mover
through the actual local cubic seed and characteristic Baumann alternative.
`EightInitialSelection` combines these branches: absence of initial selection
forces strict midpoint slices, a backward shift gives radius escape, and
the reciprocal path supplies a strict forward slice whose selected span
is nonabelian. Its final theorem gives initial selection for every original
radical image and both full covers. Only the independently owned selected
terminal contradiction and final DistanceFourInput assembly remain here;
the unequal order-sixteen and mixed terminal branches remain open.

`Terminal.SmallKernelFutureLine`, `Terminal.EightUnequalFuture` and
`Terminal.EightUnequalSecondBackward` close the source-compatible unequal
second-selector route: the first selected path is used as the future path,
and the order-two failure line is transported through the two generated
stabilizers. `Terminal.EightEqualSecondBackward` closes the equal route
using the common mover's omega center to compare the seed intersections.
`Terminal.EightExclusion` selects a concrete second strict neighbor by
transporting the first pair. The mover comparison is preserved, so the two
terminal results exclude distance four for every nonelementary radical image
of order eight, retaining both covers and the full central attachment.

`Terminal.ElementaryCubeClosure` excludes the elementary three-pair radical
image by selecting a backward critical path and comparing its paired full
kernels. `Terminal.SixteenEqualSecondBackward` closes the equal-mover
order-sixteen branch. In the unequal branch, `SixteenUnequalSeedCentrality`
identifies the neighboring seed intersection with the edge omega center and
centralizes the first backward commutator. `SixteenUnequalFailureLine`
places the second failure line in the original edge seed; its required
first-mover fixedness remains unproved.

`Terminal.MixedGeneratedLine` puts the full mixed backward kernel in every
midpoint conjugate and centralizes the orbit span. `MixedSpanCenter`
identifies that kernel with the selected seed's intersection with the span
center, with exact index two. `MixedSpanDerived` identifies the span's
derived subgroup with the critical line of order two and proves it fixed
by the starting stabilizer. Its terminal application retains the endpoint
commutator and opposite residual-core estimates explicitly.

Source: MS93 (5.12), printed pp.924--927.
-/
